+#### Docker jobs: detached on the machine
+
+A [Docker](Docker) job (update, rollback, stack deployment, scan,
+clean-up, restic backup/check/prune/restore — `DockerJob`,
+[`app/tasks/docker_jobs.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/tasks/docker_jobs.py))
+can outlive the worker that started it — on purpose, because the
+container being replaced may be one debcontrol's own host needs.
+
+- **Detached** ([`app/ssh/detached_jobs.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/ssh/detached_jobs.py),
+  the mechanism [updates](Updates) use): the worker starts the job's
+  script with `setsid nohup` and reads its log every 3 s. Log, exit
+  status, pid and boot id live under `~/.local/state/debcontrol/docker/`
+  of the connecting account (mode 600) and are removed once the result is
+  recorded. The script arrives on standard input and is kept in the job's
+  own file, never on a command line, since it may carry a restic password
+  or a Git token.
+- **Resumed**: the following task refreshes `heartbeat_at` every 20 s;
+  `reconcile_docker_jobs` (Beat, every minute, and 150 s after a worker
+  starts) claims running jobs quiet for 2 minutes and reads the same
+  files again — finished, still running, or gone because the machine
+  rebooted (boot id changed). A job is claimed with conditional
+  `UPDATE`s and its log file is created exclusively, so it never runs
+  twice.
+- **One at a time per machine**: a job moves from pending to running only
+  under a row lock on its machine while nothing else runs there; when it
+  ends, the machine's oldest pending job is queued. A pending job nobody
+  started is nudged by the same sweep and given up after 24 hours.
+- **The security model.** debcontrol's containers never mount the Docker
+  socket: Docker is reached over SSH through the machine's own `docker`
+  CLI and the same grant as monitoring. The scanners (Trivy, Grype),
+  restic, git and curl run as throwaway containers *on the managed
+  machine*, labelled `io.debcontrol.helper=1`; only the scanners and the
+  container-recreate helper get the machine's Docker socket (the scanners
+  read-only). Git tokens, `.env` files and restic credentials are
+  `encrypt_secret`ed, never shown back, and reach git/restic through a
+  mode-600 env file; a stack's webhook token is stored only as its
+  SHA-256 and `/hooks/docker/<token>` can do nothing but deploy that
+  stack from its configured source. On debcontrol's own host, updates,
+  rollbacks, stack deployments, Compose actions other than *start* and
+  restores that touch debcontrol's own Compose project are refused.
+
