+# 🐳 Docker
+
+*A machine's Docker tab: containers with their charts, updates that put the old container back when the new one fails, image vulnerability scans with Trivy and Grype, Compose stacks written in debcontrol or pulled from Git, a shell and a file browser inside containers and volumes, clean-up, and restic backups with restore.*
+
+## In practice
+
+- A machine on which debcontrol found the `docker` CLI gets a **Docker**
+  tab (`/machines/{id}/docker`), even when the account can't reach the
+  daemon yet - the tab then says how to grant it. Its sub-pages are
+  **Containers**, **Stacks**, **Images**, **Volumes**, **Networks**,
+  **Backups** and **Jobs**.
+- **Update a container** from its page, or tick several and *Update
+  selected* / *Update all with a newer image*. The new image is pulled,
+  optionally scanned, and the container recreated with the same settings;
+  if it doesn't stay up, the old one is put back.
+- **Automatic updates** are a per-container switch plus a scheduled task
+  *Update Docker containers* (see [Scheduling](#scheduled-tasks)).
+- **Scan images** with Trivy and Grype on the Images page; a
+  **vulnerability gate** can stop an update whose new image is worse.
+- **Stacks**: write a `compose.yaml` (and `.env`) in debcontrol, or point
+  at a Git repository and let it deploy on every push.
+- **Backups**: back up volumes and host paths with restic to a local path
+  or S3/B2/Azure/GCS/Swift/REST, check the repository, prune to a
+  retention, restore a snapshot.
+- Everything long runs as a **job** on the machine and keeps going if
+  debcontrol restarts; the Jobs page shows its live output.
+
+The container table and the three Docker charts used to be on the
+[Monitoring](Monitoring) tab; since 0.119.0 that tab only shows how many
+containers there are and links here.
+
+## Containers
+
+The **Containers** page is the container list of the latest monitoring
+sample (how it is collected: [Monitoring](Monitoring#docker-containers)):
+name, CPU, memory, network rate, ports, image, status and the newer image
+version if one exists, plus each container's auto-update badge, last
+update job and the vulnerability counts of its image. Above it are the
+**Docker CPU, memory and network charts** per container, with the same
+time-range picker and drag-to-zoom as the Monitoring tab.
+
+- *Start*, *Stop*, *Restart* (`action.power`) work as before
+  (`POST /machines/{id}/containers/{name}/{action}`, audited as
+  `machine.container.<action>`).
+- *Check image updates* (`machine.manage`) runs the registry-digest
+  comparison described on [Monitoring](Monitoring#docker-containers).
+- *Update selected* / *Update all with a newer image* (`action.updates`)
+  queue one update job per container; they run one after another.
+
+**A container's page** (`/machines/{id}/docker/containers/{name}`) shows
+the container as `docker inspect` describes it right now - image, state,
+command, restart policy, ports, networks and mounts (a volume links to its
+file browser) - with the names of its environment variables only: their values
+never leave the machine, since they usually hold secrets. From there:
+*Update*, *Roll back*, the update settings, *Shell* and *Files*.
+
+### Updating a container
+
+An update is one job in two steps
+([`app/ssh/docker_update.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/ssh/docker_update.py)):
+
+1. **Pull** the reference the container was created from (`nginx:1.27`).
+   Pulling changes nothing that runs. If the image id did not change, the
+   job ends as **skipped** ("already the newest"). When a vulnerability
+   gate is set, the new image is scanned here (and the running image too,
+   for the *worse* gate, if it has no scan yet).
+2. **Recreate**, only when the image changed and the gate let it through:
+   - a container Compose created is recreated by Compose itself
+     (`docker compose up -d --no-deps <service>` with the project's own
+     files and directory), so the project stays the one source of truth;
+   - any other container is recreated from its own `docker inspect`: the
+     same configuration minus what came from the old image's defaults (so
+     the new image's defaults apply), with every volume kept - anonymous
+     ones too - and reconnected to all its networks with their aliases
+     and fixed addresses. It is created through the Docker Engine API
+     with curl in a helper container, because the CLI can't create a
+     container from an inspect record.
+
+The old container is renamed to `<name>-debcontrol-old` (not removed)
+until the new one is up. The new one has to be running, not restarting
+and with a restart count of 0 after **15 seconds**; otherwise its last 40
+log lines go into the job's output, it is removed and the old one is
+renamed back and started (for a Compose service: the previous image is
+tagged again and the service brought up on it). The job then fails with
+"the previous one was put back".
+
+Not updatable, and the job says why: a container created from a fixed
+image id or digest (there is no newer image of its tag), and one whose
+image was built on the machine (nothing to pull).
+
+**Roll back** recreates the container on the image it ran before its
+last successful update or rollback - the same recreate step, after
+tagging that image with the container's reference again. It needs that
+image to still be on the machine; a clean-up of *every image no container
+uses* removes it.
+
+### Automatic updates and the vulnerability gate
+
+Each container has two settings (`action.updates`, table
+`docker_container_settings`):
+
+- **Update automatically** - the container is picked by the scheduled
+  task *Update Docker containers* with the scope "Those set to update
+  automatically". Nothing updates on its own without such a task.
+- **Vulnerability gate** - what stops its updates
+  ([`app/services/docker_gate.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/services/docker_gate.py)):
+
+| Gate | The update is stopped when |
+|---|---|
+| `off` | never (no scan) |
+| `critical` | the new image has a critical vulnerability |
+| `high` | the new image has a critical or high vulnerability |
+| `worse` | the new image has more critical + high vulnerabilities than the running one |
+
+A manual update can choose a gate for that one run (the default is the
+container's own setting); a scheduled task can use each container's
+setting or one gate for all.
+
+The gate **fails closed**: when it is on and neither scanner could scan
+the new image (or, for `worse`, the running image has no scan to compare
+with), the update is stopped - "could not check" never counts as
+"nothing found". A stopped update changes nothing: the tag is pointed back
+at the running image so nothing deploys the new one by accident, the job
+ends as **blocked** with the reason, and the *Docker update blocked*
+notification fires.
+
+## Images and vulnerability scans
+
+The **Images** page reads the machine live: every image with its size,
+whether a container uses it, and its vulnerability counts, plus Docker's
+disk-usage summary. *Scan* one image, or a scope:
+
+| Scope | Scans |
+|---|---|
+| *Scan images in use* (`in_use`) | every image a container uses |
+| *Scan new images* (`unscanned`) | images in use that have no scan yet |
+| *Scan all images* (`all`) | every image on the machine |
+
+At most 200 images per scan job.
+
+**How a scan works**
+([`app/ssh/docker_scan.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/ssh/docker_scan.py)):
+
+- **Both scanners, always the newest.** Trivy (`aquasec/trivy:latest`)
+  and Grype (`anchore/grype:latest`) run in throwaway containers on the
+  machine. Each scan pulls both images first (without internet it falls
+  back to the copy already there), and each refreshes its vulnerability
+  database, kept between runs in the named volumes
+  `debcontrol-trivy-cache` and `debcontrol-grype-cache`.
+- **One list.** A finding reported by both scanners for the same
+  vulnerability id, package and installed version is one finding; it
+  keeps the higher severity and says which scanners saw it. Grype's
+  "negligible" counts as low. If one scanner fails, the other's findings
+  are kept and the failure is shown with the scan.
+- **Little leaves the machine**: each scanner prints one line per finding
+  through a template instead of its full JSON report.
+- The scanners see the images through the Docker socket, mounted
+  read-only, and get no other access to the machine.
+
+A scan is stored per image id (`docker_image_scans`, up to 2,000
+findings, most severe first); scans of images no longer on the machine
+are dropped. **Vulnerabilities** of an image
+(`/machines/{id}/docker/images/{image_id}/vulnerabilities`) lists them
+with package, installed and fixed version and a link, filterable by
+severity and searchable by id or package.
+
+The totals over the images the machine's containers use are kept on the
+machine (`Machine.docker_vulnerabilities`) and feed the notification
+conditions `docker.critical_vulnerabilities` and
+`docker.high_vulnerabilities`.
+
+### Clean-up (prune)
+
+The Images page's **Clean up** (`action.updates`) shows how many stopped
+containers and images each option would remove, then runs Docker's own
+prune commands as a job and reports the space reclaimed
+([`app/ssh/docker_prune.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/ssh/docker_prune.py)):
+
+| Option | Removes |
+|---|---|
+| Stopped containers | `docker container prune` |
+| Untagged images | dangling images only (`docker image prune`) |
+| Every image no container uses | `docker image prune -a` |
+| Unused networks | `docker network prune` (never the built-in ones) |
+| Unused anonymous volumes | `docker volume prune` |
+| Also unused named volumes | `docker volume prune -a` - **data**: needs the machine's name typed |
+| Build cache | `docker builder prune` |
+
+A volume or image a container uses is never removed - that is Docker's
+own rule. A **scheduled** clean-up never removes named volumes.
+
+## Volumes and networks
+
+**Volumes** lists every volume with its driver, which containers use it
+and - on request, since Docker has to measure them - its size. Each
+volume has a file browser (`action.terminal`). **Networks** lists the
+networks with their driver, scope and the containers attached.
+
+## Shell and files inside a container
+
+**Shell** (`action.terminal`) opens a shell inside the container: the
+machine terminal's WebSocket with `?container=<name>`, which runs
+`docker exec -it` into it (bash if the image has it, else sh) as root
+inside the container - the same as `docker exec` by hand. It is the
+[machine terminal](Terminal-Logs-and-Runbooks)
+in every other respect: same permission, same time limit, same recording,
+and audited as `machine.container.shell.open` / `.close`. Like the
+terminal it is web-only. It needs a confirmed host key.
+
+**Files** (`action.terminal`) browses, views, edits, downloads, uploads,
+creates folders and deletes - in a container or in a volume
+([`app/ssh/docker_files.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/ssh/docker_files.py)):
+
+- **In a container**, listing, new folders and deleting run inside it
+  with `docker exec`, so it must be running and have a shell (a
+  "distroless" image has none). Reading and writing go through
+  `docker cp`, which works on a stopped container too.
+- **In a volume**, everything runs in a throwaway busybox container with
+  no network and only that volume mounted at `/data` - read-only for
+  listing and reading. Paths are kept inside the volume.
+- Limits: 2,000 entries per folder, text edited up to 256 KiB, downloads up
+  to 50 MiB, uploads up to 25 MiB, enforced on the machine. A file keeps
+  its owner and mode when it is saved.
+- Every download and every change is audited (`machine.docker.file.*`).
+
+## Stacks
+
+The **Stacks** page has two parts: the stacks debcontrol deploys itself,
+and every Compose project found on the machine.
+
+### Compose projects on the machine
+
+Any Compose project - started by hand, by another tool, or by debcontrol
+- is listed with its containers and the directory and files it was
+started from (read from `docker compose ls` and its containers' labels).
+
+| Action | Permission | What it runs |
+|---|---|---|
+| Start, stop, restart | `action.power` | `docker compose start` / `stop` / `restart` |
+| Down | `action.power` | `docker compose down --remove-orphans` - containers and networks, never volumes |
+| Up | `action.updates` | `docker compose up -d --remove-orphans` with the project's own files |
+| Pull & recreate | `action.updates` | `docker compose pull`, then `up -d` |
+| View its compose files | `action.terminal` | reads the files (up to 512 KiB each) |
+
+Which files a project uses is always read from the machine, never taken
+from the request: `up` with a file of one's choosing could start any
+container with any mount, which is more than `action.updates` grants.
+
+### Stacks debcontrol deploys
+
+A **stack** (`action.terminal` to create or edit, table `docker_stacks`)
+has a name - the Compose project name: lowercase letters, digits, `_` and
+`-`, up to 63 characters, kept once the stack exists - and one source:
+
+- **Editor**: a `compose.yaml` written in debcontrol (it must be YAML with
+  a `services` section; the rest is checked by `docker compose config` on
+  the machine) and an optional `.env`.
+- **Git**: an **HTTPS** repository, a branch (default `main`), the compose
+  file's path inside it (default `compose.yaml`) and, for a private
+  repository, an access token (a token given as `user:token` sets the
+  user name, otherwise `x-access-token` is used). The `.env` is still
+  written in debcontrol and never goes into the repository. Only
+  `https://` addresses without credentials in them are accepted.
+
+The compose file and the `.env` are at most 512 KiB each.
+
+**Deploying** (`action.updates`; *Deploy*, or *Deploy right after saving*
+on the form) writes the
+stack's files to `~/.local/share/debcontrol/stacks/<name>/` of the
+account debcontrol connects as - `compose.yaml` and `.env`, or the
+repository shallow-cloned into `repo/` plus `.env` - then runs
+`docker compose config`, `pull` (unless asked not to) and
+`up -d --remove-orphans`. Git runs in a helper container
+(`alpine/git:latest`) as the same account. The stack page shows the
+deployed commit, when it was deployed, whether the stored settings
+changed since, and its recent jobs.
+
+**Deleting** a stack takes it down, deletes its directory on the machine
+and forgets it. Its named volumes go too only when asked for, with the
+stack's name typed. A stack that was never deployed is simply forgotten.
+
+### GitOps: auto-sync and the webhook
+
+A Git stack is kept up to date in two ways:
+
+- **Auto-sync** (a switch on the stack): every 5 minutes debcontrol asks
+  the repository for the branch's newest commit (`git ls-remote`, no
+  clone) and deploys it when it isn't the deployed one, or when the stored
+  settings changed. *Check Git now* (`action.updates`) does the same at
+  once.
+- **Webhook** (`action.terminal` to create, replace or remove): debcontrol
+  makes a URL `https://<debcontrol>/hooks/docker/<token>` for the Git host
+  (GitHub, GitLab, Gitea...) to call on a push:
+
+  ```bash
+  curl -fsS -X POST https://debcontrol.example.com/hooks/docker/<token>
+  ```
+
+  The URL is **shown once**, when it is made, and stored only as the
+  token's SHA-256 - make a new one if it is lost. It is the whole
+  credential, so it works without a session and outside the sign-in
+  network allowlist, but all it can do is have that one stack pulled from
+  its own configured repository and branch and deployed. The request body
+  is ignored; what is deployed never comes from the caller. An unknown
+  token answers 404; calls less than 10 seconds apart, and calls while a
+  deployment is already waiting or running, are accepted (202) without
+  queuing another. Audited as `machine.docker.stack.webhook_deploy` by
+  "Git webhook (automatic)".
+
+## Backups (restic)
+
+A **backup** (`docker_backups`) backs up Docker volumes and host paths with
+[restic](https://restic.net/), run in restic's own container
+(`restic/restic:latest`, pulled fresh for every job) on the machine -
+nothing to install
+([`app/ssh/restic.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/ssh/restic.py)).
+
+Its settings (`action.terminal`):
+
+- **Repository**: a local path on the machine (mounted into the
+  container), or one of restic's remote backends that need nothing but
+  environment variables: `s3:`, `b2:`, `azure:`, `gs:`, `swift:`,
+  `rest:`. `sftp:` and `rclone:` would need keys or configuration inside
+  the container and are not offered. The repository is created on the
+  first run if it doesn't exist.
+- **Password** and **extra environment** (`KEY=value` lines, e.g.
+  `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...`). Left empty on edit, both keep what is
+  stored; *Remove the saved variables* (`clear_env: true` in the API)
+  removes the environment.
+- **Volumes** and **host paths** - at most 50 together; `/`, `/proc`,
+  `/sys`, `/dev` and `/run` can't be backed up - and exclude patterns.
+- **Stop the containers using the volumes** while the backup runs (a
+  database's files are only consistent while it is stopped). They are
+  started again when the job ends, whether it succeeded or not.
+- **Retention**: keep last / daily / weekly / monthly. With one set, every
+  backup run ends with `restic forget --prune`.
+- **Run on schedule**: off leaves it out of the scheduled backup tasks;
+  it can still be run by hand.
+
+Each volume is mounted read-only at `/backup/volumes/<name>` and each host
+path at `/backup/paths/<path>`; snapshots are tagged `debcontrol` and
+`dc-<backup id>` and recorded under the machine's name as host.
+
+| Button | Permission | Does |
+|---|---|---|
+| Back up now | `action.updates` | `restic backup` (+ forget/prune with a retention) |
+| Check repository | `action.updates` | `restic check`, optionally reading 10 % of the data |
+| Prune | `action.updates` | `restic forget --prune` to the retention (needs one) |
+| Snapshots | `machine.view` | the backup's snapshots, read live from the repository |
+| Restore | `action.terminal` | restores a snapshot - see below |
+
+**Restoring** puts a snapshot back where it came from: pick the snapshot
+and which of the backup's volumes and paths, and type the machine's name
+(it overwrites live data). The containers using those volumes are always
+stopped first and started again afterwards. Files created since the
+snapshot stay unless *Also delete files that weren't in the snapshot* is
+ticked.
+
+**Deleting** a backup only forgets it in debcontrol; the repository and
+its snapshots are left as they are.
+
+The time of the last successful backup is kept on the machine
+(`Machine.docker_backup_ok_at`) for the `docker.backup_age_hours`
+notification condition; a failed run fires *Docker backup failed*.
+
+## Jobs
+
+Updates, rollbacks, stack deployments and actions, scans, clean-ups and
+the backup jobs are `DockerJob`s. The **Jobs** page lists a machine's last
+100 with status, who started them and when; a job's page shows its output
+live.
+
+- **One job at a time per machine.** The rest wait as *pending* and start
+  in turn, so two updates never replace containers at the same time and
+  an "Update all" goes container by container. The same job (kind and
+  target) is never queued twice.
+- **Statuses**: pending, running, succeeded, failed, **blocked** (stopped
+  by the vulnerability gate, nothing changed) and **skipped** (nothing to
+  do, e.g. the image was already the newest).
+- **Time limits**: 20 minutes for a rollback, 30 for a stack action, 45
+  for an update or a stack deployment, 1 hour for a clean-up, 2 hours for
+  a scan, 3 for pruning a backup, 6 for a backup check, 12 for a backup
+  or a restore. A job pending for 24 hours without starting is given up.
+- **Kept** for 90 days, with the last 200,000 characters of output.
+- **They survive debcontrol restarting** - see
+  [Architecture](Architecture#docker-jobs-detached-on-the-machine).
+
+## debcontrol's own containers
+
+On the machine debcontrol itself runs on, the Compose project its own
+containers belong to is protected: updating, rolling back or recreating
+one of its containers, deploying a stack of that name, any Compose action
+on it except *start*, and restoring onto its volumes are refused - they
+would stop debcontrol in the middle of the job. Update debcontrol from
+**Settings → Update** instead. Starting, stopping and restarting a single
+container stay possible. Such containers carry a badge on the Containers
+page; a refused attempt is audited as *denied*, like a typed confirmation
+that didn't match.
+
+## Scheduled tasks
+
+Six actions on [Scheduling](Scheduling-and-Power#docker-actions), each
+for a machine, a group or all machines (`scheduling.manage`); machines
+without a confirmed host key or without Docker access are skipped:
+
+- **Update Docker containers** (`docker_update`) - the containers set to
+  update automatically, those with a newer image, or all; with each
+  container's own vulnerability gate or one for all.
+- **Scan Docker images** (`docker_scan`) - images in use, not yet
+  scanned, or all.
+- **Clean up Docker** (`docker_prune`) - stopped containers, untagged
+  images, unused networks and the build cache, optionally every unused
+  image or unused anonymous volumes. **Never named volumes.**
+- **Docker backup** / **Check Docker backups** / **Prune Docker backups**
+  (`docker_backup`, `docker_backup_check`, `docker_backup_prune`) - every
+  backup marked *Run on schedule*; pruning skips those without a
+  retention.
+
+## Notifications
+
+Five events ([Notifications](Notifications#events)) and three condition
+fields:
+
+| Event | Fires when | Placeholders |
+|---|---|---|
+| `docker.update.succeeded` | a container update finished | `{container}` |
+| `docker.update.failed` | a container update failed (or was put back) | `{container}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.update.blocked` | the vulnerability gate stopped an update | `{container}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.stack.failed` | deploying a stack, or an action on a Compose project, failed | `{stack}`, `{details}` |
+| `docker.backup.failed` | a restic backup run failed | `{backup}`, `{details}` |
+
+Conditions: `docker.critical_vulnerabilities`,
+`docker.high_vulnerabilities` (no match before the first scan) and
+`docker.backup_age_hours` (no match while the machine has no successful
+backup).
+
+## Permissions
+
+The Docker tab adds no permission of its own; it uses the existing ones,
+the same on the web pages and in the REST API
+([`machines_docker_common.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/web/routes/machines_docker_common.py)).
+
+| Permission | On the Docker tab |
+|---|---|
+| `machine.view` | every page: containers, charts, images and scans, volumes, networks, stacks, backups and their snapshots, jobs |
+| `action.power` | start / stop / restart a container; start / stop / restart / down a Compose project |
+| `action.updates` | update and roll back containers, auto-update and vulnerability-gate settings, image scans, clean-up, deploy a stack, up / pull & recreate a Compose project, *Check Git now*, back up now, check and prune backups |
+| `action.terminal` | the container shell, the file browser, a stack's compose file and `.env` (create, edit, delete a stack), Git sources and the webhook, a project's compose files, backup settings and restores |
+| `machine.manage` | *Check image updates* (as before) |
+| `scheduling.manage` | the scheduled Docker tasks |
+
+`action.terminal` is the line for anything that decides *what* runs or
+*what* is read and written on the machine: a compose file can mount any
+host path into a container, and a restore overwrites data.
+
+## Security notes
+
+- **No Docker socket in debcontrol.** debcontrol's own containers never
+  mount `/var/run/docker.sock`. Everything goes over SSH to the machine's
+  own `docker` CLI - plain `docker` for an account in the `docker` group,
+  or `sudo -n docker` through the grant onboarding adds (root-equivalent,
+  like the `apt-get` grant; see
+  [Machine Requirements](Machine-Requirements)).
+- **Names are validated, then quoted.** Every container, image, volume,
+  network and project name is checked against what Docker accepts before
+  it reaches a command line, and shell-quoted on top of that; nothing
+  starting with `-` reaches docker as a name.
+- **Helper containers.** Scans, backups, Git, recreating a container and
+  browsing a volume use throwaway containers (`aquasec/trivy`,
+  `anchore/grype`, `restic/restic`, `alpine/git`, `curlimages/curl`,
+  `busybox`, all `:latest` from Docker Hub), removed when they end and
+  labelled `io.debcontrol.helper=1`. The machine needs to reach Docker
+  Hub for those features.
+- **Secrets.** Git tokens, `.env` files, restic repository addresses,
+  passwords and extra environment are encrypted at rest
+  (`encrypt_secret`) and never shown again in full - a backup's
+  repository only with credentials redacted; leaving a password or token
+  field empty on edit keeps the stored one. Webhook tokens are stored only
+  as their SHA-256. A job's script, which can carry those secrets, is
+  sent on SSH standard input and kept in a mode-600 file of the job -
+  never on a command line, which every user of the machine could see in
+  `ps`; restic and git get theirs from a mode-600 env file that is removed
+  again.
+- **Typed confirmations**: removing unused named volumes and restoring a
+  backup need the machine's name; deleting a stack with its volumes needs
+  the stack's name. A wrong name is audited as a refusal.
+- Every action is CSRF-protected and audited (below).
+
+## Audit log
+
+| Code | When |
+|---|---|
+| `machine.container.start` / `.stop` / `.restart` | a container started, stopped, restarted |
+| `machine.container.shell.open` / `.close` | a shell inside a container |
+| `machine.docker.check_images` | *Check image updates* |
+| `machine.docker.container.update` | an update started (or refused for debcontrol's own container) |
+| `machine.docker.container.rollback` | a rollback started (or refused) |
+| `machine.docker.container.settings` | auto-update / gate settings changed |
+| `machine.docker.images.scan` | a scan started |
+| `machine.docker.prune` | a clean-up started (or refused: name not typed) |
+| `machine.docker.stack.create` / `.edit` / `.delete` | a stack created, changed, deleted |
+| `machine.docker.stack.deploy` | a deployment started |
+| `machine.docker.stack.action` | an action on a Compose project started (or refused) |
+| `machine.docker.stack.sync` | *Check Git now* |
+| `machine.docker.stack.webhook` | a webhook created, replaced or removed |
+| `machine.docker.stack.webhook_deploy` | a webhook call started a deployment |
+| `machine.docker.backup.create` / `.edit` / `.delete` | a backup's settings created, changed, deleted |
+| `machine.docker.backup.run` / `.check` / `.forget` | back up, check, prune started |
+| `machine.docker.backup.restore` | a restore started (or refused) |
+| `machine.docker.file.download` / `.write` / `.mkdir` / `.delete` | the file browser |
+
+A scheduled run is recorded by the scheduler, like every other scheduled
+action. How a job ended is on the Jobs page and in the notifications, not
+in the audit log.
+
+## REST API
+
+`/api/v1/machines/{machine_id}/docker/...`
+([`api_v1_docker.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/web/routes/api_v1_docker.py))
+mirrors the tab with the same permissions, service calls and audit codes.
+The full list is on [REST API](REST-API#docker).
+
+- Long operations answer **202** with `{"job": {...}}`; follow it with
+  `GET .../docker/jobs/{job_id}` (which includes the output). **409**
+  when the same job is already waiting or running, or the target is
+  debcontrol's own.
+- Typed confirmations become `confirm`: the machine's name for
+  `POST .../prune` with `all_volumes` and for `POST .../backups/{id}/restore`;
+  `DELETE .../stacks/{id}?remove_volumes=true&confirm=<stack name>`.
+- Secrets are write-only: a stack's Git token and a backup's password,
+  repository and environment never come back (`has_git_token`; the
+  repository only redacted). A stack's compose file and `.env` are
+  returned only to `action.terminal`. A webhook URL is in the answer of
+  `POST .../stacks/{id}/webhook` only.
+- `POST .../stacks` and `PUT .../stacks/{id}` with `"deploy": true` also
+  deploy, which needs `action.updates` as well; without it (or without a
+  confirmed host key) the request is refused before anything is saved.
+- Files travel base64-encoded (`GET`/`PUT .../files/content`); too large
+  is 413.
+- **Web-only**: the container shell - an interactive WebSocket, like the
+  machine terminal.
